      "math_academy_id": "q-74122",
      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "Given the vectors $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, find the distance $\\text{d}(x,y).$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the formula for the distance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{y}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{y}_{4})^{2}+({x}_{5}-{y}_{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-1)^{2}+(-1-2)^{2}+(-4-(-2))^{2}+(0-1)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9+4+1+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
